#
# **Overview.** Under one simulated noise model, our protection raised the fidelity F of a Bell pair between two distant qubits on all three processor graphs, keeping at least {{ pct([c[3], c[6], c[9]] | map(attribute="acceptance") | min) }} of shots and using fewer two-qubit gates than our baseline, the SWAP circuit. [Section {{ sec("s5-1") }}]({{ ref("s5-1") }}) gives each graph's numbers.{{ chk() }}
#
# - **The task:** create the Bell state $\lvert\beta_{00}\rangle = (\lvert 00\rangle + \lvert 11\rangle)/\sqrt{2}$, which the challenge writes $\lvert\Phi^+\rangle$, between two fixed qubits, the ports, on three processor graphs, and measure what error detection with postselection buys and costs.
# - **Our protection:** GHZ disentangling moves the pair by preparing a GHZ state along the path between the ports and disentangling it with gates, which leaves the path's interior qubits in $\lvert 0\rangle$. The vacancy read then reads those qubits at the end of the circuit and discards each shot where any reads 1. It detects errors and corrects none.
# - **The noise:** one model from IBM's Boston calibration medians, the same on every graph, simulated with {{ "{:,}".format(B.shots) }} shots per basis. Every result holds for this one model.
